//! Web APIs and the WebIDL binding layer.
//!
//! It holds the binding installer (M2 task 3) and the implementations of
//! the core Web APIs (M2 tasks 4-5): `rt::Runtime::new` registers DOM,
//! events, timers, Location/History/Navigator, Storage, URL, console,
//! XHR/fetch, encoders, DOMParser and MutationObserver in a [`Registry`].
//!
//! # Pieces
//!
//! - [`desc`]: descriptor types (pure data, engine-neutral).
//! - [`generated::chromium`]: the Chrome 153 table, generated by
//!   `lb-webidl-gen` from the Chromium IDL and checked against the
//!   captured Chrome surface. Do not edit; regenerate with
//!   `cargo run -p lb-webidl-gen -- --family chrome`.
//! - [`Registry`] (chrome): Rust implementations by interface and member.
//! - [`Bindings`] (chrome): defines every interface of the table on an
//!   `lb_js::Engine`, falling back to stubs for unregistered members, and
//!   creates window realms.
//! - [`DomExceptions`] (chrome): throwable `DOMException`s for host
//!   functions.
//! - `rt` (chrome): the implementations and the per-frame `rt::Window`
//!   state the page layer drives (tasks, timers, parser, documents).
//! - [`snapshot`] (chrome): the process-wide V8 startup snapshot of the
//!   bindings (templates plus a window context), made in memory on first
//!   use; engines restored from it create window realms in about a
//!   millisecond.
//! - [`host`]: engine-neutral types the page layer provides (network,
//!   storage areas, console messages, encoding sniffing).
//! - [`text`]: `innerText`-like text without layout.
//!
//! The firefox build compiles only the descriptor types and tables until
//! SpiderMonkey lands (M4b).
//!
//! # Use (chrome)
//!
//! ```ignore
//! let mut engine = Engine::new(persona.clone(), EngineOptions::default())?;
//! let mut reg = Registry::new();
//! let dom = reg.dom_exceptions();
//! reg.getter("Node", "nodeType", host_fn(|cx| {
//!     let node = cx.this_host().ok_or_else(|| HostError::type_error("Illegal invocation"))?;
//!     cx.ret(node_type(node))
//! }));
//! reg.method("Node", "appendChild", host_fn(move |cx| {
//!     /* ... */ Err(dom.error("HierarchyRequestError", "..."))
//! }));
//! // Once per engine: define all interfaces (the table follows the
//! // persona's family). Then one realm per frame / isolated world.
//! let bindings = Bindings::define(&mut engine, &reg)?;
//! let realm = bindings.new_window_realm(&mut engine, WindowRealmOptions {
//!     window_host: Some(HostRef::new(WINDOW_KIND, frame_id)),
//!     ..Default::default()
//! })?;
//! // DOM nodes map 1:1 to wrappers through lb-js's per-realm cache:
//! let div = bindings.interface_id("HTMLDivElement").unwrap();
//! engine.wrap(realm, div, HostRef::new(NODE_KIND, node_id))?;
//! ```
//!
//! [`install_window_realm`] does both steps for a single realm.
//!
//! # Host refs
//!
//! Wrappers carry an `lb_js::HostRef { kind, id }` chosen by the page layer
//! (e.g. kind = node, id = arena index). This crate reserves no kinds:
//! `DOMException` keeps its state in private properties of each object.
//!
//! # Registration rules
//!
//! - Prototype, instance, global (`Window`) and namespace (`CSS`) members:
//!   [`ImplKind::Getter`] / [`ImplKind::Setter`] / [`ImplKind::Method`]
//!   under the interface or namespace name.
//! - Static members: `StaticGetter` / `StaticSetter` / `StaticMethod`.
//! - Constructors: [`Registry::constructor`]; bind the new object with
//!   `cx.init_this(host)`. Without one, `new X()` throws `Illegal
//!   constructor`.
//! - `[LegacyFactoryFunction]`s: [`ImplKind::LegacyFactory`] under the
//!   element interface with the factory name as member (`"HTMLImageElement",
//!   "Image"`); return the element wrapper.
//! - `toString` of a `stringifier attribute x` defaults to x's getter.
//! - Iterables over an indexed getter use `Array.prototype` functions and
//!   need only the indexed getter and `length`.

